# PRODUCTION LOCK: INFINITY PROTOCOL v6.2

## 1. THE MANDATE
This file enforces Project Context Isolation. Before ANY destructive command (`deploy`, `delete`, `drop`), the system MUST verify the active Firebase project mathematically matches the authorized production target.

## 2. THE `safe-deploy-guard.sh` SCRIPT
Every project MUST implement this bash script and run it as a `predeploy` hook in `firebase.json` or `package.json`.

```bash
#!/bin/bash
# scripts/safe-deploy-guard.sh

AUTHORIZED_PROJECT="ccceoscott" # Extracted from MISSION_STATE.md

echo "Checking deployment locks..."

# 1. Get currently active Firebase project
ACTIVE_PROJECT=$(firebase use)

# 2. Compare against authorized project
if [[ "$ACTIVE_PROJECT" != *"$AUTHORIZED_PROJECT"* ]]; then
  echo "❌ CRITICAL: Active Firebase project ($ACTIVE_PROJECT) does not match authorized target ($AUTHORIZED_PROJECT)"
  echo "HALTING DEPLOYMENT to prevent cross-contamination."
  exit 1
fi

# 3. Directory sanity check
if [[ ! -f "firebase.json" ]]; then
  echo "❌ CRITICAL: Not in project root. Missing firebase.json."
  exit 1
fi

echo "✅ Deployment Locks: PASS. Target: $AUTHORIZED_PROJECT"
exit 0
```

## 3. PACKAGE.JSON ENFORCEMENT
Tie the lock script to standard NPM verbs to prevent accidental bypasses.

```json
{
  "scripts": {
    "predeploy": "bash scripts/safe-deploy-guard.sh",
    "deploy": "firebase deploy --only hosting,functions",
    "deploy:rules": "npm run predeploy && firebase deploy --only firestore:rules"
  }
}
```

## 4. AGENT VERIFICATION COMMAND
If you are asked to deploy or execute a destructive mutation manually, you MUST run this inline verification first:
```bash
firebase use && head -n 10 MISSION_STATE.md | grep "Project ID"
```
If you detect a mismatch, HALT immediately and drop execution.
